course: dictionary
lesson: 9071
title: Slit: a long, narrow cut or opening
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A slit is a long, narrow cut or opening. You can also slit something, which means to make that kind of cut in it. Slit can be a noun or a verb, so look at the words around it.
explain: Context gives you clues. «A female red-billed hornbill seals herself into a tree hole, and the male feeds her through a narrow slit.» The word narrow tells you a slit is thin, not wide. You can also use the phrase «slit open», which means cut open along a long, narrow line.
explain: Be careful when you slit things. Use scissors or a blunt letter opener, and ask an adult for help if you are unsure. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: does «slit the envelope open» still make sense? If it does, you have it.
fact: A slit is a long, narrow cut or opening.
fact: To slit something means to make a long, narrow cut in it.
fact: «Most sharks have five gill slits on each side.» Gill slits are narrow openings in the side of the body.
fact: «A cat's pupils shrink to thin slits in bright light.» A pupil is the dark middle of the eye.
fact: «Foxes ... have vertical slit pupils like cats.»
exercise: «The male fed the mother hornbill through a narrow slit.» What is a slit? | *A long, narrow opening | A very big hole in the ground | A loud noise | A kind of bird
exercise: Maya will slit the envelope open with a blunt knife. What does slit mean here? | To put it in a box | To lick it closed | To weigh it on the scales | *To make a long, narrow cut in it
exercise: Which animal has vertical slit pupils, like a cat? | *A fox | A monkey | A hornbill | A lion
test: «The wall had a narrow slit for the letter box.» What does the word slit tell you about the letter box opening? | *It is long and narrow | It is made of gold | It is hidden under the floor | It is wide and round
test: Sam says, «I will slit the tape to open the box.» What is Sam going to do? | Stick the box together | Paint the tape red | *Make a long, narrow cut in the tape | Weigh the box
exam: A sharp-eyed scientist sees «a narrow slit» of light coming through a door, and asks what slit means here. | A big round window | *A long, narrow opening, like a thin gap | A shiny metal box | A long, narrow opening
